WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:10.030
RL: We're always accustomed to thinking of inheritance as what

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:12.880
happens in terms of the nuclear genome that we

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.260
inherit from our mother and the nuclear genome that we inherit from our father.

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:21.850
But what you will see is that, with mitochondria, the particular complement

00:00:21.850 --> 00:00:25.990
that is inherited from your mother can be quite different depending

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.180
on the egg that is fertilized.

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:34.790
So if we have a primordial egg cell and here we

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:41.580
have the nucleus of the egg cell, this particular egg may have,

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:45.930
let's say, three mitochondria in pink that

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:51.790
carry some sort of defect in their mitochondrial genomes.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:57.920
And then it may have, let's say, the equivalent

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:03.540
of six mitochondria that are perfectly healthy.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:07.340
What happens that, is in the process, of differentiation,

00:01:07.340 --> 00:01:12.500
of maturation of the eggs, when this undergoes cell division

00:01:12.500 --> 00:01:20.330
and you end up with, let's say, two eggs,

00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:34.070
you can have a situation where you have a distribution of mitochondria where,

00:01:34.070 --> 00:01:38.540
in this case, the cell on the right only received

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:43.570
one of the diseased mitochondria while the cell on the left

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.410
actually received two.

00:01:46.410 --> 00:01:50.770
What this means is that the quantity, the relative ratio

00:01:50.770 --> 00:01:55.920
of normal to defective mitochondria is quite different

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:58.990
if you compare these two egg cells.

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.350
So what this means is that, depending on which one is fertilized,

00:02:02.350 --> 00:02:07.670
the resulting embryo may have either a significantly greater number

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:13.040
of diseased mitochondria or defective mitochondria versus less.

00:02:13.040 --> 00:02:17.760
And so this actually helps determine whether or not the individual in

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:23.340
question-- the resulting child that is produced from that egg--

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:27.740
whether or not that child will express the disease or not express the disease.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:31.860
Or if it does express the disease, how severely affected

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:34.660
they might be by the mitochondrial disease.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:38.770
So what this means is that mitochondria and their distribution,

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:44.620
their ratio in a particular egg cell in one woman can be quite variable.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:48.920
And depending on what egg is fertilized and, thus, what embryo is then

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.440
developed, you have a chance that you can have a defect

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:56.120
or you might not have a defect at all.

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:00.290
With mitochondrially-inherited diseases, there is, in fact,

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:05.560
a threshold of defects that can occur-- that you have the inheritance of far

00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:08.450
more than just two or three mitochondria,

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.400
you have the inheritance of several mitochondria.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:15.620
And what we can see now are situations in which, let's say, you

00:03:15.620 --> 00:03:18.883
have three different eggs.

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:25.230
And I'm not going to show the nuclear DNA because that's not

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:27.200
what we're interested right now.

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:32.350
You might have one egg that has three of the mitochondria that

00:03:32.350 --> 00:03:34.680
have the defect, so in pink.

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:38.330
Another one just has one, and another one has two.

00:03:41.320 --> 00:03:44.950
Let's say, for the sake of simplicity, all of the other mitochondria,

00:03:44.950 --> 00:03:47.900
I will use one color to denote them.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:48.652
Let's do purple.

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:55.620
So here, we have another three.

00:03:55.620 --> 00:03:57.850
Another three.

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:02.270
And let's, for the sake of argument, have another three.

00:04:02.270 --> 00:04:09.820
Now, if you compare these three different eggs, what you'll see

00:04:09.820 --> 00:04:19.300
is that, in this case, 50% of the mitochondria that that egg has

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:20.790
are defective.

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:21.480
Right?

00:04:21.480 --> 00:04:26.510
In this case, we're down to 25% are defective.

00:04:26.510 --> 00:04:28.920
One pink to three purple.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:37.290
And in this case, we see that 40% are defective.

00:04:37.290 --> 00:04:41.980
What is observed is that typically, with many mitochondrial diseases,

00:04:41.980 --> 00:04:46.830
if you're in the range of 25% to 40% being defective,

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:52.330
you still have enough normal mitochondria to generate enough ATP.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:58.730
So quite often, these individuals are normal or healthy.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:07.030
In this case, what we might have is a situation where

00:05:07.030 --> 00:05:13.140
50%-- or in fact, what's often the case, 50% to 60%

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:21.065
of mitochondria having the defect can often result in a disease presentation.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:32.100
So what this means is that you can have siblings in the same family.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:37.420
You might have one sibling where the egg that was fertilized

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:43.760
had 25% of the mitochondria carrying the defect while another egg was fertilized

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:48.130
for a different sibling that had 50% or more.

00:05:48.130 --> 00:05:53.680
This individual up here will show the disease phenotype

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:57.920
while this individual will be entirely healthy.

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:02.740
And so this is one of the remarkable observations with mitochondrial disease

00:06:02.740 --> 00:06:06.690
is that, even within the same family, even with siblings, you

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:12.730
can see variations in whether or not the disease is seen, is presented

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:16.660
or not so that one way of thinking about it

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:24.240
is that there is a threshold, if you will,

00:06:24.240 --> 00:06:30.800
of the mitochondrial mutation that must be present for the disease

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.950
to display itself.

00:06:32.950 --> 00:06:36.450
So that, interestingly enough, you may not have realized this,

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:39.120
but for every egg that is fertilized, it's

00:06:39.120 --> 00:06:42.330
a little bit of sort of a one-arm bandit where

00:06:42.330 --> 00:06:46.980
you pull the lever, three things come up, and, in some cases,

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:51.230
unfortunately, if three defects come up or a high enough percentage

00:06:51.230 --> 00:06:56.790
of mitochondria are defective, you cross the threshold for disease.

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:58.890
So this is a very different way of thinking

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:03.869
about how disease might be inherited not through the nuclear genome

00:07:03.869 --> 00:07:05.410
but through the mitochondrial genome.

00:07:11.710 --> 00:07:13.260
[MUSIC PLAYING]

